{"id":"u-c5879fcb-d63d-429b-9318-e3acded1fab5","src":"community","title":"FORGED 5","author":"Swanson311","url":"https://www.boostcamp.app/users/mshdgR-forged-5","desc":"Progressive PPL Hypertrophy Program\n\nFORGED 5 is a 5-day Push / Pull / Legs training program designed to build muscle, increase strength, and improve body composition through consistent training and progressive overload.\n\nThis program isn't about simply showing up and completing the same workouts every week. The goal is to gradually become stronger, more capable, and more muscular over time.\n\nTHE FORGED 5 PHILOSOPHY\n\nTrain hard. Recover. Progress. Repeat.\n\nThe exercises and rep ranges provide the framework, but your progression is what drives the results.\n\nYou don't need to destroy yourself every workout or add weight every single session. Instead, focus on gradually improving your performance while maintaining good technique.\n\n---\n\n🔥 PROGRESSIVE OVERLOAD\n\nProgressive overload means gradually increasing the demands placed on your muscles over time.\n\nYour body adapts to the work you give it. If you continue doing exactly the same amount of work forever, eventually your body has less reason to adapt.\n\nProgress can come from:\n\n- Increasing weight\n- Increasing repetitions\n- Improving technique and range of motion\n- Performing the same work with better control\n- Occasionally increasing training volume\n\nThe primary method in FORGED 5 is DOUBLE PROGRESSION.\n\nFor example, if an exercise calls for:\n\n3 × 8–12 reps\n\nYou might begin with:\n\n70 lb: 10 / 9 / 8\n\nOver the following weeks, your goal is to gradually increase your repetitions:\n\n70 lb: 11 / 10 / 9\n70 lb: 12 / 11 / 10\n70 lb: 12 / 12 / 11\n70 lb: 12 / 12 / 12\n\nOnce you can reach the top of the prescribed rep range on all sets with good form, increase the weight.\n\nFor example:\n\n70 lb → 75 lb\n\nYour repetitions may drop:\n\n75 lb: 9 / 8 / 8\n\nThat's completely normal.\n\nNow you work your way back toward the top of the rep range.\n\nThe cycle is:\n\nBuild reps → reach the top of the range → increase weight → build reps again → repeat.\n\nThat's progressive overload.\n\n---\n\n🧱 TRAINING INTENSITY\n\nMost working sets should finish with approximately 1–3 reps in reserve (RIR).\n\nRIR means how many additional good-quality repetitions you believe you could have performed at the end of a set.\n\n3 RIR: You could have done about 3 more reps.\n2 RIR: You could have done about 2 more reps.\n1 RIR: You probably had 1 good rep left.\n0 RIR: You reached muscular failure.\n\nYou don't need to take every set to failure.\n\nFor most compound exercises, staying around 1–3 RIR will allow you to train hard while maintaining performance and recovery.\n\nFor isolation exercises, the final set can occasionally approach 0–1 RIR.\n\n---\n\n📈 KEEP A TRAINING LOG\n\nIf you aren't tracking your workouts, it's difficult to know whether you're actually progressing.\n\nRecord:\n\n- Exercise\n- Weight\n- Repetitions\n- Sets\n- Optional RIR\n\nYour goal isn't to beat everyone else in the gym.\n\nYour goal is to gradually beat your previous numbers.\n\nA workout log turns FORGED 5 into a measurable progression rather than simply a collection of workouts.","level":["Intermediate"],"goals":["Strength","Muscle"],"equipment":["Full Gym"],"minutes":60,"joins":0,"rate":0,"rateCount":0,"updated":"2026-10-04T12:00:56.676Z","variations":[{"name":"default","days":["Push A","Pull A","Legs A","Push B","Pull B"],"weeks":[[{"ex":[{"n":"Bench Press (Barbell)","sets":[{"lo":5,"hi":8,"x":4}]},{"n":"Incline Bench Press (Dumbbell)","sets":[{"lo":8,"hi":12,"x":3}]},{"n":"Seated Shoulder Press (Dumbbell)","sets":[{"lo":6,"hi":10,"x":3}]},{"n":"Lateral Raise (Cable)","sets":[{"lo":12,"hi":20,"x":3}]},{"n":"Chest Fly (Cable)","sets":[{"lo":10,"hi":15,"x":3}]},{"n":"Tricep Pushdown (Cable)","sets":[{"lo":10,"hi":15,"x":3}]},{"n":"Overhead Tricep Extension (Dumbbell)","sets":[{"lo":12,"hi":15,"x":2}]}]},{"ex":[{"n":"Lat